The "Windows embeddable package (64-bit)" you're referring to is likely the Python embeddable package for Windows 64-bit systems. This package is designed for developers who want to embed Python into their applications or use Python as a scripting language in their projects. It contains the essential components of Python, including the Python interpreter, standard library, and other necessary files, but excludes some non-essential tools and libraries that are typically included in the full Python installation.

The embeddable package is more lightweight and easier to distribute with your application compared to the full Python installation. It can be useful in scenarios where you want to minimize the size of your application or when you need to ensure a specific version of Python is used with your application.

To use the embeddable package, you can follow these steps:

1. Download the package from the official Python website: <https://www.python.org/downloads/windows/> (look for the "Windows x86-64 embeddable zip file" under the "Python 3.x.x" section).
2. Extract the contents of the zip file to a directory in your application or project.
3. To run Python scripts, you can use the `python.exe` file located in the extracted directory. For example, to run a script named `my_script.py`, you can use the following command:

   ```
   path\to\extracted\directory\python.exe my_script.py
   ```

4. To use Python as a scripting language in your application, you can call the `python.exe` file with the appropriate arguments from your code. For example, in C++, you can use the `system()` function to execute Python scripts:

   ```cpp
   #include <cstdlib>

   int main() {
       std::string command = "path\\to\\extracted\\directory\\python.exe my_script.py";
       system(command.c_str());
       return 0;
   }
   ```

Keep in mind that the embeddable package does not include some tools and libraries that are part of the full Python installation, such as the IDLE development environment, pip package manager, and some third-party libraries. If you need these tools or libraries, you may need to install them separately or use the full Python installation instead.

